            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_s\\mid s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for exactly one \\(s\\).  \nThus they cover \\(S_n\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is always attainable.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  From a covering with many sunny lines to one with fewer  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k_0\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose any line \\(\\ell\\) that does **not** meet any point of \\(S_n\\) (for instance the vertical line \\(x=n+10\\)).  \nAdding \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and, if necessary, discarding a non‑sunny line from \\(\\mathcal L\\) yields a new family of \\(n\\) lines that still covers \\(S_n\\) but now has \\(k_0+1\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence, **once a covering with \\(k_0\\) sunny lines exists, coverings with every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(k_0\\le k\\le n\\) also exist.**  \nTherefore the problem reduces to the existence of a covering with the *maximum* possible number of sunny lines, namely \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A covering with all lines sunny  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+2-i). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals the integer \\(i\\); for all \\(i\\ge1\\) this slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Distinctness.*  Different values of \\(i\\) give different slopes, therefore the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Coverage of \\(S_n\\).*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+1 .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(i\\) satisfies (5), so \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the line \\(L_i\\) is defined.  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = i a + (n+2-i)\n   = (b-a+1)a + n+2-(b-a+1)\n   = ba - a^2 + a + n+1-b\n   = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\) was arbitrary, the union \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\) contains all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are **all sunny** and together cover the required set.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining every value of \\(k\\)\n\n* For \\(k=0\\) we use the diagonal covering (3).  \n* For any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we start from the all‑sunny covering \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) and replace arbitrarily \\(n-k\\) of its lines by the diagonal lines \\(D_s\\) (which are non‑sunny).  \n  Because each diagonal line already contains points of \\(S_n\\), the covering property is preserved.  \n  After the replacement we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
