r all \\(|C_j|=n+1-j\\) points of \\(C_j\\) we need at least \\(n+1-j\\) distinct lines among \\(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{x=i_0\\}\\).\n\n   Summing this lower bound over all \\(j\\neq i_0\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F|\\;=\\;n\\;\\ge\\;1+\\sum_{j\\neq i_0}(n+1-j)\n   =1+\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}(n+1-j)\\Bigr)- (n+1-i_0)\n   =1+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n+1-i_0).\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n+i_0-2\\le 2n-2,\n   \\]\n\n   which is impossible for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) (the left\u2011hand side grows quadratically while the right\u2011hand side is linear).  \n   Hence the assumption that both a sunny and a non\u2011sunny line appear in a covering family is false.\n\n   Consequently any covering family of \\(n\\) lines consists **either** entirely of sunny lines **or** entirely of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   By the constructions of \u00a72 the two possibilities correspond precisely to \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=0\\).\n\n   Therefore no integer \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n\\) can be realised.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is *non\u2011sunny* (horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus a covering with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y=t\\,x+(n+1-t). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(t\\); therefore \\(L_t\\) is sunny because \\(t\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical.  \nThe lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n*Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Since \\(a\\ge1\\) we may rewrite the equation of \\(L_t\\) as  \n\n\\[\nb=t a+(n+1-t)\\iff\nt=\\frac{b-n-1}{a-1}+1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), i.e. \\(b-n-1\\le -a\\). Substituting this inequality into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{-a}{a-1}+1=\\frac{1}{a-1}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand \\(b\\ge1\\) gives \\(b-n-1\\ge -n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{-n}{a-1}+1\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the right\u2011hand side of (2) is a real number belonging to the interval \\([1,n]\\).\n\nWrite \\(a-1=d\\) (\\(d\\ge0\\)). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb=n+1-a-(n+1-a-b)=n+1-a-(\\text{non\u2011negative})\\;,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(b-n-1\\equiv -d\\pmod{d}\\). Therefore the fraction \\(\\dfrac{b-n-1}{a-1}\\) is an integer; adding \\(1\\) we see that the expression in (2) is an integer.  \nConsequently there exists a unique integer \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) satisfying (2), and for this \\(t\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\). \u220e\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the family \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and consists solely of sunny lines; therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No mixed configurations (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing both a sunny line and a non\u2011sunny line.  \nWrite \\(\\mathcal F=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\ge1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\in S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the *\\(i\\)-th column*; \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim 1  \nIf a line is not vertical, it meets any column \\(C_i\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives a single value of \\(y\\); hence at most one point of the column lies on the line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line, there must be a column that is covered completely by a **single** line of \\(\\mathcal N\\); otherwise every non\u2011sunny line would be horizontal or diagonal, each of which meets every column in at most one point, contradicting the fact that \\(|C_i|>1\\) for some \\(i\\).  \nHence there exists an index \\(i_0\\) such that the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{i_0}:\\;x=i_0\\in\\mathcal N\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole column \\(C_{i_0}\\).\n\nConsider any other column \\(C_j\\) with \\(j\\neq i_0\\).  \nThe line \\(V_{i_0}\\) does not intersect \\(C_j\\); by Claim\u202f1 every remaining line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) meets \\(C_j\\) in at most one point.  \nTherefore to cover all \\(|C_j|=n+1-j\\) points of \\(C_j\\) we need at least \\(n+1-j\\) distinct lines among \\(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{V_{i_0}\\}\\).\n\nSumming these necessary numbers over all \\(j\\neq i_0\\) we obtain a lower bound for the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nn=|\\mathcal F|\n   \\ge 1+\\sum_{j\\neq i_0}(n+1-j)\n   =1+\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}(n+1-j)\\Bigr)-(n+1-i_0)\n   =1+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n+1-i_0).\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n+i_0-2\\le 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the left\u2011hand side exceeds the right\u2011hand side (the left side grows like \\(n^2/2\\) while the right side is at most \\(2n-2\\)). This contradiction shows that a covering family cannot contain both a sunny and a non\u2011sunny line.\n\nConsequently any covering family of \\(n\\) lines consists **either** entirely of sunny lines **or** entirely of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nThe constructions of Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 show that both possibilities are realizable, and no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Answer\n\nThe only admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e"
