e point.*  \n*If a line is not horizontal, it meets any row in at most one point.*  \n*If a line is not of slope \\(-1\\), it meets any anti‑diagonal in at most one point.*\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting the fixed \\(x\\)- (resp. \\(y\\)-, resp. \\(x+y\\)-) coordinate into the linear equation leaves at most one solution for the remaining coordinate, unless the line is parallel to the corresponding direction. ∎\n\n---\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line. Write \\(\\mathcal F=\\mathcal N\\cup\\mathcal S\\) where \\(\\mathcal N\\) is the set of non‑sunny lines and \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of sunny lines; both sets are non‑empty.\n\nWe treat three possibilities for a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n#### (i)  A vertical line belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nLet this line be \\(x=v\\). Then the whole column \\(C_v\\) (size \\(n+1-v\\)) is covered by this single line. For any other column \\(C_j\\) (\\(j\\neq v\\)) the vertical line does not meet it, and by Lemma 1 each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines can contribute at most one point to \\(C_j\\). Consequently we need at least \\(|C_j|\\) of the remaining lines to cover \\(C_j\\). Summing over all \\(j\\neq v\\) we obtain the lower bound\n\n\\[\nn\\;\\ge\\;1+\\sum_{j\\neq v}|C_j|\n   =1+\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}|C_j|\\Bigr)-|C_v|\n   =1+\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-v).\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n-2\\), which is false for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence a vertical line cannot appear in \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n#### (ii)  A horizontal line belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nThe same argument applied to rows (using Lemma 1) yields the identical contradiction. Thus no horizontal line can belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n#### (iii)  A diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nNow the anti‑diagonal \\(A_d\\) (size \\(d-1\\)) is covered by this line. For any other anti‑diagonal \\(A_s\\) (\\(