und for the number of double\u2011counted points; any actual configuration can only have fewer double counts, so (2) is a valid inequality.)\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even*, then \\(t=\\frac{n}{2}\\) and the right\u2013hand side of (2) equals\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2=\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\]\n   which is far smaller than \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Hence an all\u2011sunny covering cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)). Then \\(t=m+1\\) and (2) gives\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le n(m+1)-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n          = (2m+1)(m+1)-\\frac{(2m+1)2m}{2}\n          = m+1 .\n   \\]\n   But \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=m(2m+1)+m+1>m+1\\) for every \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   Consequently an all\u2011sunny covering is also impossible for odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   The only odd value not excluded by the above computation is \\(n=3\\) (\\(m=1\\)). For \\(n=3\\) the bound (2) becomes\n   \\[\n   |S_3|=6\\le 3\\cdot2-\\binom{3}{2}=6,\n   \\]\n   so the inequality does not rule out the existence of an all\u2011sunny configuration. Indeed such a configuration exists (see \u00a76).\n\n6. **Explicit all\u2011sunny configuration for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, none is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), and they contain respectively the point sets\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,4),(2,3)\\},\n   \\]\n   which together give exactly the six points of \\(S_3\\). Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable (non\u2011sunny diagonal lines).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also realizable (the three sunny lines above).  \n   * For any \\(n\\ge4\\) a covering family must consist entirely of sunny lines (mixed families are impossible) and an