011sunny line, then all its lines are non\u2011sunny.}}\n   \\tag{Lemma\u202f2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **All\u2011sunny families are impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Assume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each line contains at most \\(t=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Two distinct lines intersect in at most one point, so the total number of *distinct* points covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le nt-\\binom{n}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (The term \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) is an upper bound for the number of double\u2011counted points; removing it can only increase the right\u2011hand side, so inequality (2) is valid.)\n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \n   Then \\(t=m\\) and the right\u2011hand side of (2) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   2m\\cdot m-\\frac{2m(2m-1)}2=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S_{2m}|=m(2m+1)>m\\) for \\(m\\ge2\\), (2) is violated.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge5\\)).*  \n   Here \\(t=m+1\\) and (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_{2m+1}|\\le (2m+1)(m+1)-\\frac{(2m+1)2m}{2}=m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(|S_{2m+1}|=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=(2m+1)(m+1)>m+1\\).  \n   Hence (2) cannot hold.\n\n   Consequently an all\u2011sunny covering does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{If }n\\ge4\\text{, a covering family cannot consist solely of sunny lines.}}\n   \\tag{Lemma\u202f3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) we have \\(t=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{4}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=2\\).  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), and they contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1\\cap S_3=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n   L_2\\cap S_3=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\n   L_3\\cap S_3=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The six points above are exactly the set \\(S_3\\). Hence for \\(n=3\\) a covering family with \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * By Lemma\u202f2 a covering family is either completely non\u2011sunny (giving \\(k=0\\)) or completely sunny.  \n   * Lemma\u202f3 shows that a completely sunny covering is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   * The explicit construction of \u00a72 gives a non\u2011sunny covering for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   * The explicit construction of \u00a76 shows that a completely sunny covering exists for \\(n=3\\).  \n\n   Therefore the only attainable values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\\qquad\n   k=n\\quad\\text{only when }n=3 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For all }n\\ge3:\\;k=0\\text{ is possible; }k=n\\text{ is possible only for }n=3.}\n\\]"

