            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd},\\;1\\le k\\le n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists precisely when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **A useful reduction.**  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   (Any other choice of non‑sunny lines can be transformed into this one by a suitable affine change of coordinates that preserves the notions “sunny’’ and “non‑sunny’’.)  \n   These vertical lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n   The points that remain uncovered form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem:\n\n   > **Sub‑problem.** For a given integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) decide whether the triangular set \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) *sunny* lines.\n\n   If the answer for \\(k\\) is “yes’’ then, for any \\(n\\ge k\\), we obtain a covering of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines by adding the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   Conversely, any covering of \\(S_n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a covering of \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines (the reduction above).  \n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) for the original problem are precisely the admissible values for the sub‑problem.\n\n3. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   For an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate is integer iff  \n\n   \\[\n   q\\,b\\equiv -p\\,x\\pmod q .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the integer points of \\(L\\) occur for \\(x\\) in an arithmetic progression with step \\(|q|\\).  \n   Inside the strip \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) there are at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   of them, because \\(|q|\\ge 1\\) and the inequality \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) forces \\(|q|\\ge 2\\) unless the slope is an **integer** (\\(|q|=1\\)).  \n\n   *If the slope is an integer* \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+m)a\\le k+1-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|1+m|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible integer abscissae is again at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n\n   Consequently every sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}\n   \\tag{Lemma 1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n4. **All‑sunny coverings exist exactly for odd \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 2 (necessity).*  \n   If \\(k\\) is even, then by Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(\\frac{k}{2}\\) points of \\(S_k\\).  \n   With \\(k\\) lines we can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}2 < \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |S_k|\n   \\]\n\n   points, a contradiction. Hence an all‑sunny covering of \\(S_k\\) is impossible for even \\(k\\).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (sufficiency).*  \n   For every odd integer \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\) there exists an explicit covering of \\(S_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The construction is by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n   *Base \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \n   The single line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) covers the unique point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n   *Base \\(m=1\\) (\\(k=3\\)).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x +5,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   cover the set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume we have a covering of \\(S_{2m-1}\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines, each of which contains exactly \\(m\\) points (the bound of Lemma 1 is attained).  \n   To obtain a covering of \\(S_{2m+1}\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n   1. **Shift the old covering.**  \n      Translate every point of the old covering one unit to the right, i.e. replace \\((a,b)\\) by \\((a+1,b)\\).  \n      The translated set is exactly  \n\n      \\[\n      \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le 2m+2\\}=S_{2m+1}\\setminus R,\n      \\]\n\n      where  \n\n      \\[\n      R=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le 2m+1\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 2