\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   The case \\(k=0\\) is realised by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines\n   \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).\n\n3. **A bound for a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line and write it in reduced rational form  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   For an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate is integer exactly when  \n\n   \\[\n   q\\,b\\equiv -p\\,x\\pmod q .\n   \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the integer points of \\(L\\) occur for \\(x\\) in an arithmetic progression\n   with step \\(|q|\\).  \n   Inside the strip \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) there are at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{3.3}\n   \\]\n\n   of them, because \\(|q|\\ge2\\) unless the slope is an integer.\n   If the slope is an integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) then (3.1) becomes \\(y=mx+b\\) with\n   \\(b\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \n   For a point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) we have \\(y\\le k+1-x\\); substituting gives  \n\n   \\[\n   mx+b\\le k+1-x\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(m+1)x\\le k+1-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|m+1|\\ge2\\), the same estimate (3.3) follows.  \n   Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n          \\text{ points of }T_k.}\n   \\tag{Lemma\u202f1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Necessity of oddness (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   Assume that \\(T_k\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points,\n   so the total number of covered points satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (4.1) forces \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\);\n   consequently \\(k+1\\) is even, i.e. **\\(k\\) i