  Summing the contributions of all lines we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t=0}^{m}(m+1-t)+\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}(m+1+t)\n   =\\bigl[(m+1)+(m)+\\dots +1\\bigr]+\\bigl[(m+1)+(m+2)+\\dots + (2m)\\bigr]\n   =\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}{2}=|T_k|.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) appears exactly once; the families (4.1)\u2013(4.2) give a partition of \\(T_k\\) into \\(k=2m+1\\) sunny lines, each line containing precisely \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}=m+1\\) points.\n\n   This proves\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{For every odd }k\\text{ there exists a covering of }T_k\\text{ by }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\\tag{Lemma\u202f3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) cover all required points.  \n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   \u2013 Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   \u2013 On the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f3.  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).*  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently a configuration with an even positive number of sunny lines does not exist.\n\n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those stated in the boxed answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{ or }\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\((0\\le k\\le n)\\).\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (1).  \nConsequently the points that are still uncovered are\n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for \\(T_n\\) **iff** the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nFor \\(k=0\\) we may simply use the \\(n\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHenceforth we study the following problem.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge1\\) decide whether there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(T_k\\).  \nWrite its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (otherwise the line would be non\u2011sunny).\n\n*If \\(|q|\\ge2\\).*  \nFor an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate given by (2) is integer exactly when  \n\n\\[\nqx\\equiv -qb\\pmod q .\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step \\(|q|\\ge2\\).  \nInside the interval \\(1\\le x\\le k\\) there are at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nvalues, and each such \\(x\\) yields a unique point of \\(L\\cap T_k\\).\n\n*If \\(|q|=1\\) (i.e. the slope is an integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\)).*  \nThen (2) is \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(b\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nFor two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\) on \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\n(x_1+y_1)-(x_2+y_2)=(1+m)(x_1-x_2).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) we have \\(|1+m|\\ge2\\); consequently the sums \\(x+y\\) of two distinct points differ by at least \\(2\\).  \nSince the admissible sums range from \\(2\\) up to \\(k+1\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(L\\) can belong to \\(T_k\\).\n\n*If \\(L\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_k\\)* the bound is trivial.\n\nThus every sunny line meets \\(T_k\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Lemma\u202f1: }|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ for any sunny line }L.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessity of oddness (Lemma\u202f2)\n\nThe cardinality of the triangle is  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\), Lemma\u202f1 yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Even \\(k\\).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\) and the right\u2011hand side of (4) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); contradiction.  \nHence no covering exists for even \\(k>0\\).\n\n*Odd \\(k\\).*  \nWrite \\(k=2m+1\\). Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (4) becomes an equality. Consequently each of the \\(k\\) lines must contain exactly \\(m+1=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThus a covering is possible only for odd \\(k\\) (or \\(k=0\\)).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Lemma\u202f2: }k\\text{ must be odd (or }k=0\\text{).}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f3)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \nDefine the following \\(k\\) lines.\n\n*Family\u202fA (slope \\(\\tfrac12\\)).*  \nFor each \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,m\\) set  \n\n\\[\nA_t:\\; y=\\frac12x+\\frac12+t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Family\u202fB (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \nFor each \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nB_t:\\; y=-2x+(4m+3)-2t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(m=0\\) the family\u202fB is empty, leaving only the single line \\(A_0\\).)\n\nAll these lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(\\frac12\\) and \\(-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Points on the lines of Family\u202fA\n\nWrite \\(x=2s+1\\) with \\(s\\ge0\\). Substituting into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac12(2s+1)+\\frac12+t=s+1+t .\n\\]\n\nHence the integer points of \\(A_t\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(2s+1,\\;s+1+t),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,m-t .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each admissible \\(s\\),\n\n\\[\n(2s+1)+(s+1+t)=3s+2+t\\le3(m-t)+2+t\\le2m+2=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso every point in (7) belongs to \\(T_k\\).  \nThe number of points on \\(A_t\\) is \\((m-t)+1=m+1-t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Points on the lines of Family\u202fB\n\nRewrite (6) as \\(y=-2x+C_t\\) with \\(C_t=4m+3-2t\\).  \nFor integer \\(x=s\\) (where \\(1\\le s\\le m+1+t\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny=-2s+4m+3-2t .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer points of \\(B_t\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(s,\\;4m+3-2t-2s),\\qquad s=1,2,\\dots ,m+1+t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums satisfy  \n\n\\[\ns+(4m+3-2t-2s)=4m+3-2t-s\\le4m+3-2t-(1)=2m+2=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso all points in (8) lie in \\(T_k\\).  \nThe number of points on \\(B_t\\) is \\(m+1+t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The partition of \\(T_k\\)\n\nCollecting the contributions of all lines we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{t=0}^{m}|A_t|\n   &=\\sum_{t=0}^{m}(m+1-t)= (m+1)+(m)+\\dots +1 =\\frac{(m+1)(m+2)}2 ,\\\\[2mm]\n\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}|B_t|\n   &=\\sum_{t=0}^{m-1}(m+1+t)= (m+1)+(m+2)+\\dots +(2m)=\\frac{(m+1)(3m)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two sums gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(m+1)(m+2)}2+\\frac{(m+1)(3m)}2\n   =\\frac{(m+1)(4m+2)}2\n   =\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2\n   =|T_k|.\n\\]\n\nTherefore every point of \\(T_k\\) appears **exactly once** in the list (7)\u2013(8); the \\(k=2m+1\\) lines \\(A_0,\\dots ,A_m,B_0,\\dots ,B_{m-1}\\) form a partition of \\(T_k\\).  \nEach line contains precisely \\(m+1=\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points, as required.\n\nThus for every odd \\(k\\) a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Lemma\u202f3: For every odd }k\\text{ there is a covering of }T_k\\text{ by }k\\text{ sunny lines.}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the original problem\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) satisfy the conditions.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (they cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\)).  \nOn the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f3.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k>0\\).*  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently a configuration with an even positive number of sunny lines does not exist.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{ or }\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"

