 Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\((0\\le k\\le n)\\).\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the\nlines (1).  The points that are still uncovered are\n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for \\(T_n\\) **iff**\nthe triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nFor \\(k=0\\) we may simply use the \\(n\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines\n\\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHenceforth we study the following problem.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge1\\) decide whether there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound for a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  It can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c ,\\qquad a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;\\gcd(a,b)=1,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith \\((a,b)\\neq(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)\\) (the three non\u2011sunny directions).\n\nAll integer points on \\(L\\) are obtained from a particular solution\n\\((x_0,y_0)\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+bt,\\;y_0-at),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the sums \\(s=x+y\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\ns=(x_0+y_0)+(b-a)t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(a\\neq b\\); therefore \\(|b-a|\\ge1\\).\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(k+1\\);\nan arithmetic progression with step \\(|b-a|\\) can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|b-a|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nterms.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n\\qquad\\text{(Lemma\u202f1)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Parity restriction (Lemma\u202f2)\n\nThe triangle contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\), Lemma\u202f1 gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, the right\u2011hand side of (5) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\), which is\nstrictly smaller than the left\u2011hand side; (5) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2m+1\\).  Then\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (5) becomes an equality.\nThus for odd \\(k\\) each of the \\(k\\) lines must contain exactly\n\\(m+1=\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Lemma\u202f2: }k\\text{ must be odd (or }k=0\\text{).}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for odd \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f3)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Consider the two families of sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_t &: \\; x+2y = 2m+2+t ,\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2mm]\nB_t &: \\; 2x+y = 2m+2+t ,\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,m-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\)) and the\n\\(k=2m+1\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Points on the \\(A\\)