n* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) cover\nall required points.\n\n* \\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n  \u2013 Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n  \u2013 On the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) place the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by\n    Lemma\u202f3.  \n  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every\n  point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n* \\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).  \n  Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently\n  a configuration with an even positive number of sunny lines does not exist.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
